     There is only one type of "corpse" object; `corpsenm' can't
(without using some multiple value encoding) record the fact that
the corpse is human and was also a vampire lord or whatever.  Some
corpses (pets, auto-revivers like trolls, and shopkeepers mostly,
as well as some undead) have extra data attached specifically to be
able to recreate them accurately if they get revived.  Corpses of
ordinary monsters don't bother, so you can get noticeable effects
like different experience level or swapped sex if you revive them.
That info isn't recorded with every corpse because then they could
never stack together, but I'm not sure how important that stacking
actually is for corpses.

     I think is's a bit silly that zapping an undead might kill it,
then zapping its corpse with the same wand or spell will immediately
revive it.  It used to be that when a vampire, zombie, or mummy left
a human corpse, reviving the corpse would yield a new human instead
of the original undead critter.  I liked the old way better....


